1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:17,300
Redesigning an electric airliner, on a laptop. This is an independent study of the Heart Aerospace X1. We build a model of it from public data, see how far it can really fly, and then try to make it better.

2
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:27,500
Here's what Heart has published. An all-electric demonstrator, with a strut-braced high wing, a T-tail, and four motor pods on the wing.

3
00:00:27,800 --> 00:00:35,700
The span is 32.3 meters, the length 23.2 meters, and the max takeoff weight about 11,340 kilograms.

4
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:44,300
It has four 400 kilowatt electric motors, and four 90 kilowatt-hour battery packs. That's 360 kilowatt-hours in total.

5
00:00:44,600 --> 00:01:02,500
We asked four questions. How far can it really fly on 360 kilowatt-hours? Would better batteries, or a battery built into the wing, help? Would a small front wing improve efficiency? And does a real CFD simulation agree with the quick tools?

6
00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:14,700
That front-wing idea came from a remark attributed to Elon Musk: put a small wing up front, in place of the tail. We tested that idea literally.

7
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:36,900
One important note before we start. Heart hasn't published CAD for the X1. Every number here is an estimate from an independent hobby study, built from public figures, photo measurements and engineering assumptions. It is not Heart data. And the alternative layouts are hypothetical. None of them exists on the real aircraft.

8
00:01:37,200 --> 00:01:42,300
All of it runs on one laptop, with free tools.

9
00:01:42,600 --> 00:01:55,300
OpenVSP for the geometry. AVL for quick aerodynamics and stability. Python and Octave for range, mission and battery models. And OpenFOAM for CFD, a full simulation of the airflow.

10
00:01:55,600 --> 00:01:58,800
Part 2: The model.

11
00:01:59,100 --> 00:02:12,600
Step one: build a paper airplane from public data. Every value lives in one parameter file, and every value is tagged: published, photo estimate, or assumption, each with its own uncertainty.

12
00:02:12,900 --> 00:02:25,200
A Python generator reads that one file and builds everything else: the OpenVSP geometry, the AVL input decks, a watertight STL surface for CFD, and a drag build-up.

13
00:02:25,500 --> 00:02:42,200
The missing dimensions were scaled off the published span, length and height. That gives a wing of about 60.6 square meters, with an aspect ratio of 17.2: long and skinny. The area alone is uncertain by about 15 percent.

14
00:02:42,500 --> 00:02:52,400
A quick sanity check: the model comes out 22.9 meters long, against 23.2 published. And 7.37 meters tall, against 7.3. Close enough.

15
00:02:52,700 --> 00:03:02,200
Here's the result: our own estimated geometry, in three views. Not Heart's drawings, just our best reading of the public numbers.

16
00:03:02,500 --> 00:03:17,200
We also had to assume what Heart hasn't published: cruise at 80 meters per second at 3,000 meters, and the balance point, the center of gravity, at 25 percent of the wing's mean chord.

17
00:03:17,500 --> 00:03:31,000
Now the key number: L over D, lift divided by drag. It tells you how much weight the wing carries for each unit of drag. Higher means less energy per kilometer.

18
00:03:31,300 --> 00:03:45,600
AVL gives the baseline an L over D of about 21.3, and a simple cruise-only range of about 140 kilometers. Each unit of L over D is worth about 6.6 kilometers of range.

19
00:03:45,900 --> 00:03:57,800
But 21 is optimistic. The drag estimate leaves out the landing gear, cooling, and lots of small bumps and gaps. Hold that thought. It comes back later.

20
00:03:58,100 --> 00:04:01,300
Part 3: Mission energy.

21
00:04:01,600 --> 00:04:09,500
Step two: does 360 kilowatt-hours actually get you anywhere? We modeled a full mission, at max weight.

22
00:04:09,800 --> 00:04:20,500
Ten minutes of taxi. A takeoff at 1.6 megawatts. A climb at 1.2 megawatts. Cruise at 80 meters per second. And a gliding descent.

23
00:04:20,800 --> 00:04:33,100
The pack: 250 watt-hour-per-kilogram cells, about 175 at pack level once you add the structure. So 360 kilowatt-hours weighs about 2,060 kilograms. Roughly 18 percent of the airplane.

24
00:04:33,400 --> 00:04:42,100
Because 21 is optimistic, we also tested L over D values of 18 and 16, which are more realistic.

25
00:04:42,400 --> 00:04:54,300
The results. At 3,000 meters, with the optimistic L over D and no reserve: about 106 kilometers. The climb alone uses about 260 of the 360 kilowatt-hours.

26
00:04:54,600 --> 00:05:03,700
At the more realistic L over D of 18 or 16, at that altitude, the mission can't even be completed.

27
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:10,700
Fly lower, at 1,500 meters, and you get about 122, 105, or 93 kilometers.

28
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:26,500
Now the killer. Real flights carry reserve energy, like thirty minutes of loiter. The reserve alone needs 240 to 350 kilowatt-hours. With any reserve, the mission isn't feasible, at any altitude, at any L over D.

29
00:05:26,800 --> 00:05:40,300
Flip it around: how much battery would you actually need? Here's what's installed: 360 kilowatt-hours. Just 100 kilometers, with no reserve, takes about 350 to 430, depending on L over D.

30
00:05:40,600 --> 00:05:45,700
Two hundred kilometers, with no reserve: about 580 to 720.

31
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:53,900
And 200 kilometers with a 30-minute reserve: about 845 to 1,070. Two to three times what's installed.

32
00:05:54,200 --> 00:06:05,300
And you can't just bolt on more battery. More battery means more weight, which needs more energy, which needs more battery. That's the weight spiral.

33
00:06:05,600 --> 00:06:16,700
At fixed max weight, 200 kilometers plus a reserve, at L over D 18, needs about 3,460 kilograms more battery. That wipes out the payload.

34
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:28,900
Let the airplane grow instead, and 200 kilometers with no reserve converges at about 842 kilowatt-hours, and 14.8 tonnes. With a 30-minute reserve, the design never converges.

35
00:06:29,200 --> 00:06:38,700
There's a power problem too. C rate is power relative to capacity. 1 C would empty the pack in one hour.

36
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:51,700
Takeoff at 1.6 megawatts, from 360 kilowatt-hours, is a 4.4 C peak: about 2,220 amps at 720 volts. Today's high-energy cells are rated for only 1 to 2 C.

37
00:06:52,000 --> 00:07:01,500
So the X1 makes sense as a demonstrator. And the big lesson: battery energy density, not aerodynamics, is what limits it.

38
00:07:01,800 --> 00:07:05,000
Part 4: Battery chemistry.

39
00:07:05,300 --> 00:07:20,800
Step three. If the battery is the limit, what about a better battery? We compared six chemistries at the same pack mass, about 2,057 kilograms, flying at 1,500 meters with an L over D of 18.

40
00:07:21,100 --> 00:07:32,600
LFP, the safe and cheap chemistry in many cars, is too heavy: about 62 kilometers. NMC and NCA, today's high-energy workhorses, reach about 110 and 115.

41
00:07:32,900 --> 00:07:45,600
Lithium-sulfur and solid-state land around 145. And silicon-anode cells reach about 211 kilometers. They're the only chemistry here that can also carry a 30-minute reserve, at about 96 kilometers.

42
00:07:45,900 --> 00:07:59,800
The catch: today's 450 watt-hour-per-kilogram silicon cells are rated at just 1 C, and last 150 to 300 cycles. The X1 needs a 4.4 C peak, and more than a thousand cycles.

43
00:08:00,100 --> 00:08:14,000
So our recommendation is two-track. For the demonstrator: proven, high-power NMC or NCA 21700 cells, in four independent 90 kilowatt-hour packs. They handle 4 to 5 C, and they have certification data.

44
00:08:14,300 --> 00:08:27,800
For a product: silicon-anode NMC, the only chemistry that roughly doubles range at the same mass. A realistic target is a power-balanced cell of about 350 to 400 watt-hours per kilogram.

45
00:08:28,100 --> 00:08:34,000
Keep an eye on lithium-sulfur and solid-state. And avoid LFP for propulsion.

46
00:08:34,300 --> 00:08:37,500
Part 5: The wing battery.

47
00:08:37,800 --> 00:08:52,900
Step four, a wilder idea. What if the battery is the wing? Pack cylindrical cells into the wing box, bond them with potting, and let the cells work like a honeycomb core that carries load.

48
00:08:53,200 --> 00:09:05,500
Space isn't the problem. The wing box holds about 6.4 cubic meters, and 360 kilowatt-hours needs only 17 to 20 percent of it. Mass is the real constraint.

49
00:09:05,800 --> 00:09:14,500
Dropping the separate pack structure gives a structural pack of about 1,753 kilograms, against 2,057 for a conventional pack.

50
00:09:14,800 --> 00:09:28,700
But where in the wing? This is a strut-braced wing. The strut meets the wing about 6.5 meters out, and the peak bending moment, about 397 kilonewton-meters, sits right at the strut.

51
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:38,900
Cells inboard of the strut give no bending relief at all, because that peak is set by the loads outboard of it.

52
00:09:39,200 --> 00:09:55,900
Cells outboard of the strut cut the wing moment by a third, and strut tension by 29 percent. But on landing, strut compression nearly triples, roll inertia more than doubles, and the cells are more exposed in a crash.

53
00:09:56,200 --> 00:10:05,300
Here's the beam model behind it: shear, bending moment, and where the battery mass sits, for each of five placements.

54
00:10:05,600 --> 00:10:16,300
Net savings land between about 257 and 377 kilograms, 2 to 3 percent of max weight. That's roughly 10 to 15 percent more energy.

55
00:10:16,600 --> 00:10:32,500
The surprise was the glue. Peak shear in the cell core is about 0.5 to 1.5 megapascals. Structural adhesive on bare or primed metal cans is good for 2.4 to 3.8 at design level. Plenty of margin.

56
00:10:32,800 --> 00:10:48,700
But normal cells come wrapped in plastic shrink film, and the film's own adhesive becomes the weak link: about 0.5 at design level. With wrapped cells, every placement fails. So the cells must be unwrapped and primed.

57
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:58,100
Heat matters too. Takeoff and climb warm the pack by about 30 degrees, with almost no cooling in that time.

58
00:10:58,400 --> 00:11:11,100
In cruise, a spread-out layout can shed its losses, about 18 kilowatts, passively through the wing skins. A single inboard block can only shed 13 to 14. Not enough.

59
00:11:11,400 --> 00:11:24,100
And a full-depth block gets too hot in the middle. So keep the stack about two cells deep, or add fins that carry the heat out to the skins.

60
00:11:24,400 --> 00:11:39,100
Our pick: spread the cells from the wing root out to the outboard motor pod, two tiers deep, bonded to primed cans, with fins to the skins. Net saving: about 250 to 300 kilograms.

61
00:11:39,400 --> 00:11:53,700
But be honest about the open issues: crashworthiness, venting a thermal runaway inside a wing, cell swelling, cells you can't replace one by one, flutter, and certification. No aircraft has flown with this.

62
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:57,500
Part 6: A small front wing.

63
00:11:57,800 --> 00:12:08,100
Step five: aerodynamics. Could a small front wing make the X1 more efficient? A front wing is called a canard, or a foreplane.

64
00:12:08,400 --> 00:12:23,900
Our quick tool is AVL, a vortex-lattice method. It models the lifting surfaces only: no fuselage, no viscosity, no real stall. So stall is estimated when the first strip of wing reaches an assumed lift limit.

65
00:12:24,200 --> 00:12:29,700
We tried four layouts. A is the baseline, with its T-tail.

66
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:37,900
B is a Piaggio P.180-style three-surface layout: a small foreplane, plus a tail shrunk to 60 percent.

67
00:12:38,200 --> 00:12:43,700
C is the literal version: a foreplane replaces the tail completely.

68
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:55,900
And B-opt is the best B we found in a sweep: a 4 square meter foreplane, 7 meters back, with the tail cut to 4.8 square meters.

69
00:12:56,200 --> 00:13:15,700
To compare them fairly, each one gets a 15 percent static margin. Static margin is how far the balance point sits ahead of the aerodynamic neutral point. Positive means the airplane naturally returns to level flight. And every layout is trimmed at the same cruise lift.

70
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:33,500
Layout C first. Left at the same balance point, it's unstable, with a static margin of minus 17 percent. It looks 3 to 4 percent more efficient, but only with a computer actively flying it, and it shows a deep-stall trap.

71
00:13:33,800 --> 00:13:45,300
Make it stable, and it falls apart. The smallest workable foreplane is 10 square meters, and every workable size loses 13.6 to 16.4 percent of range.

72
00:13:45,600 --> 00:14:00,700
Why? Deleting the tail saves about 5.3 percent. But trimming with a front wing ruins the lift distribution: span efficiency falls from 0.95 to 0.52, and that costs about 17.8 percent. Net: about minus 13.5.

73
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:17,700
Layout B, as first drawn, was slightly better: L over D 21.6, versus 21.3. But its wing stalled before the foreplane. That's unsafe. You want the front wing to stall first, so the nose drops and the airplane recovers.

74
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:33,100
So we swept foreplane size and position. Bigger foreplanes lost range, up to 7.5 percent. The best was small: 4 square meters, 7 meters back, plus a tail shrunk to 4.8 square meters. That's B-opt.

75
00:14:33,400 --> 00:14:42,900
B-opt versus the baseline, both at 15 percent static margin: L over D 21.60, versus 21.28. A gain of 1.5 percent.

76
00:14:43,200 --> 00:14:53,900
Good news first. B-opt's foreplane does stall first, the safe behavior. The cost is a higher stall speed: 45.1 meters per second, versus 43.0.

77
00:14:54,200 --> 00:15:13,300
The weak point is takeoff rotation. With an elevator only on the small tail, the nose wheel doesn't lift until about 54 meters per second, against a target of 49.6. Every fix, like a foreplane flap or a bigger tail, eats into that 1.5 percent.

78
00:15:13,600 --> 00:15:24,700
Engine-out handling is fine. With an outboard motor out, it needs only 8.8 degrees of rudder, versus 11.1 for the baseline. And climb is unchanged.

79
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:37,300
AVL's verdict: a canard replacing the tail is clearly worse. A small foreplane plus a smaller tail is a small, fragile gain, inside the uncertainty of our geometry.

80
00:15:37,600 --> 00:15:40,800
Part 7: CFD setup.

81
00:15:41,100 --> 00:15:56,200
AVL is fast, but it can't see the fuselage, the motor pods, the struts, viscosity, or real stall. So next: CFD, computational fluid dynamics. A full simulation of the air flowing around the whole airplane.

82
00:15:56,500 --> 00:16:09,600
We used OpenFOAM, solving the steady RANS equations with the k-omega SST turbulence model, at 80 meters per second, at 3,000-meter conditions. That's a Reynolds number of about 8.4 million.

83
00:16:09,900 --> 00:16:24,200
To save memory, we simulate only half the airplane, with a symmetry plane down the middle. And angle of attack is set by tilting the incoming air, so one mesh serves every angle.

84
00:16:24,500 --> 00:16:39,200
The space around the airplane is chopped into small cells. That's the mesh. A coarse mesh had about 1.1 million cells. A medium mesh, about 2.3 million. Finer meshes are more accurate, but slower.

85
00:16:39,500 --> 00:16:50,600
Each case ran on 6 cores, one at a time, because memory was limited to 15 gigabytes. 1,500 iterations per case, in an overnight queue.

86
00:16:50,900 --> 00:17:04,400
One snag: OpenFOAM's built-in force tools crashed in this build. So we wrote our own script, forces dot py, that adds up pressure and skin friction from the saved flow fields.

87
00:17:04,700 --> 00:17:17,000
We ran the baseline, B-opt, and the 10 square meter canard, C10, at angles from 0 to 16 degrees, with the control surfaces left at zero. So, untrimmed.

88
00:17:17,300 --> 00:17:20,500
Part 8: CFD results.

89
00:17:20,800 --> 00:17:37,900
The first coarse-mesh results were a shock. L over D around 13, instead of AVL's 21. And the wing was separating early, at just 4 to 8 degrees. Separation means the air peels off the surface instead of following it.

90
00:17:38,200 --> 00:17:45,300
So: was the airplane bad, or was the mesh bad? We checked by refining it.

91
00:17:45,600 --> 00:17:58,700
Here's the baseline at 8 degrees. Red marks reversed flow on the upper wing. On the coarse mesh, 47 percent of the wing. On the medium mesh, just 10 percent.

92
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:12,900
Lift rose from 0.915 to 1.128. Drag fell 39 percent. And L over D doubled, from 6.2 to 12.5. So most of that early separation was a mesh error, not real physics.

93
00:18:13,200 --> 00:18:19,100
Drag still isn't fully converged, so treat the absolute numbers as conservative.

94
00:18:19,400 --> 00:18:30,100
Corrected for the medium mesh, cruise L over D is about 15.0 for the baseline, and 14.9 for B-opt. Versus about 21 in AVL.

95
00:18:30,400 --> 00:18:38,300
CFD can't tell A and B-opt apart. So B-opt's 1.5 percent gain is neither confirmed nor refuted.

96
00:18:38,600 --> 00:18:56,900
Why 15, and not 21? Partly the mesh. But also real interference drag that AVL's build-up misses. At zero degrees, the struts add about 63 drag counts, the pods 75, and the fuselage 62. A drag count is one ten-thousandth of drag coefficient.

97
00:18:57,200 --> 00:19:08,300
Here's the surface pressure on the baseline at 8 degrees, on the coarse mesh. Blue is suction, low pressure. That's where the lift comes from.

98
00:19:08,600 --> 00:19:24,100
And the canard? Untrimmed, C10 looks about 4 to 5 percent better than the baseline. But to trim, it has to deflect the foreplane, and AVL says that costs about 14 percent. Trimmed, it's not better.

99
00:19:24,400 --> 00:19:37,900
CFD also confirms the foreplanes stall first, the safe order. But they lift less than AVL predicts. On B-opt, the foreplane's lift slope is only about a third of AVL's value.

100
00:19:38,200 --> 00:19:43,700
By 12 degrees, all three layouts make nearly the same lift.

101
00:19:44,000 --> 00:20:02,300
On the medium mesh, the baseline looks more stable than the coarse mesh suggested: about 74 percent of AVL's stability slope. So our earlier warning, about moving the balance point forward, was probably too pessimistic. That isn't re-checked yet for B-opt and C10.

102
00:20:02,600 --> 00:20:12,100
And the separation that remains sits just behind, and outboard of, the two places where the motor pods meet the wing.

103
00:20:12,400 --> 00:20:24,300
Pod drag barely changes with the mesh, so this may be a real feature of this pod placement. It's worth a closer look with a finer mesh.

104
00:20:24,600 --> 00:20:28,100
Part 9: What we got wrong.

105
00:20:28,400 --> 00:20:35,100
Now the honest part: what we got wrong, and what we still don't know.

106
00:20:35,400 --> 00:20:56,900
A parameter mix-up in OpenVSP made the motor pods in the first CAD export twice the intended diameter. We found it and fixed it. Measured on the CFD meshes, the pods are the right 0.8 meters, so the CFD used the correct geometry. We'd said otherwise earlier, and that was wrong.

107
00:20:57,200 --> 00:21:12,300
The mesh isn't converged: drag still moves 16 to 39 percent per refinement. One canard case, at 16 degrees, diverged and wasn't re-run. And there are no propellers, no landing gear, and no trimmed CFD.

108
00:21:12,600 --> 00:21:24,500
And underneath it all: the geometry is estimated from photos, the masses are assumptions, and the battery and structure numbers use typical datasheet values, not test data.

109
00:21:24,800 --> 00:21:28,000
Part 10: Conclusions.

110
00:21:28,300 --> 00:21:34,600
So, what can a laptop tell you about an electric airliner? Four things.

111
00:21:34,900 --> 00:21:46,800
One: the battery limits the mission. 360 kilowatt-hours gives about 105 to 125 kilometers with no reserve, and can't carry one. Silicon-anode cells are the product path.

112
00:21:47,100 --> 00:21:58,200
Two: a structural wing battery could save roughly 250 to 380 kilograms. But it needs bare, primed cell cans, and a long road to certification.

113
00:21:58,500 --> 00:22:13,600
Three: a canard replacing the tail costs about 14 percent of range. A small foreplane plus a smaller tail gains 1.5 percent in AVL, and ties in CFD. On this evidence, not worth the complexity.

114
00:22:13,900 --> 00:22:23,400
Four: real-world L over D is probably nearer 15 than 21. Interference drag matters, and the pod-wing junctions deserve design attention.

115
00:22:23,700 --> 00:22:40,000
Next steps: a finer mesh and a proper convergence study. Medium-mesh runs at high angles for B-opt and C10. Trimmed CFD, with propeller slipstream. A fairing study for the pod junctions. And real tests of the cell-potting bond.

116
00:22:40,300 --> 00:22:50,200
One last reminder: everything here is an independent estimate, built from public data. It is not Heart Aerospace data. Thanks for watching.
